  "N14" [label="(13,0,0) Side chain modifications (length, topology, branching) modulate solubility, crystallization, and stacking"];
  "N16" [label="(15,1,9) Replacing linear with branched chains regulates stacking, improving structural order and charge transport"];
  "N17" [label="(16,0,0) Breaking alkyl chain symmetry is effective for boosting photovoltaic performance"];
  "N18" [label="(17,0,0) Asymmetric acceptor A-C10ch had less energy loss than symmetric Y-C10ch"];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) PCE of OSCs has exceeded 19% due to advances in A-DA'D-A type SMAs"];
  "N20" [label="(19,0,0) Reducing electron-vibrational coupling suppresses non-radiative recombination and energy loss"];
  "N21" [label="(20,0,0) Comprehensive investigation with DFT and crystal structures is essential for understanding mechanisms"];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) OSCs have higher energy loss and lower FF compared to inorganic photovoltaics"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate Y-series A-DA'D-A small-molecule acceptors bearing asymmetric alkyl/thienyl outer side chains for PM6-based organic solar cells, testing whether backbone planarization and improved molecular packing can diminish electron-vibrational non-radiative losses, enhance charge generation and charge transport, and thereby raise power-conversion efficiency.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When prior acceptor-design advances, morphology-transport relationships, charge-generation mechanisms, energy-loss mechanisms, and structure-analysis tools all point to side-chain structure as a controllable variable, a cautious research proposal can integrate synthesis, characterization, and mechanism testing around that variable."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Side-chain engineering in A-DA'D-A small-molecule acceptors can regulate stacking-related molecular organization and charge-transport-relevant structural order."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Lower electron-vibrational coupling is a plausible mechanism to investigate for the reduced energy loss observed or expected in asymmetric acceptor structures."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence that side-chain engineering controls stacking and charge-transport-relevant order, together with prior benefits from breaking alkyl-chain symmetry, motivates systematic investigation of asymmetric side-chain acceptors for packing and photovoltaic-performance effects."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) DFT calculations and crystal-structure analysis are appropriate tools for probing mechanisms underlying asymmetric side-chain effects in acceptor materials."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The paper is motivated to develop and evaluate asymmetric side-chain A-DA'D-A/Y-series small-molecule acceptor strategies, combining device, photophysical, morphological, charge-dynamics, DFT, and crystal-structure analyses to test how side-chain modulation affects packing, charge generation, charge transport, energy loss, and power-conversion efficiency."];
